  "provenance": {
    "keyboard_indices": "US func_0808F358 uses seven row pointers and copies two bytes for each 16-bit glyph. Native JP func_0808F358 at 0808F15C branches on keyboard kind 0/1/2 and loads row pointers from 09EBC12C/09EBC148/09EBC164; its row cursor is clamped to 0..6. Native EU function at 0808EBF0 selects 09F6FE6C for kind 2 and 09F6FE8C otherwise; C cursor logic proves respective row domains 0..7 and 0..6, and native code copies one byte. The old EU gUnk_09EE4B0C raw alias at 09F6FE30 denotes graphics, not a keyboard index, and is removed without inventing a common table.",
    "encoded_assets": "Keyboard and default deck text remain binary-backed assets. US utf16le denotes exact little-endian 16-bit game glyph storage, preserving musical, gender, arrow and bracket glyph code units. JP uses Shift-JIS byte pairs, including full-width spaces and punctuation. EU singlebyte is a game glyph encoding: bytes 01..17 and 83..97 in symbol rows are selectable glyph codes, not text transformations or ASCII normalization. Every binding ends at its first native zero terminator. Byte-view declarations for keyboard payloads match the native byte-copy consumer.",
    "default_deck_names": "US and JP SetDeckName callers use their three native encoded Deck 1/2/3 literals. EU active callers already use separately owned LocalizedText wrappers at 09F65FDC/09F65FF0/09F66004; the three adjacent English single-byte literals 09F6FD74/7B/82 are coherent unused native duplicates and get honest EU-only asset names, not false mappings to the US UTF-16 names. US text is 42 bytes followed by two alignment zero bytes; EU text is 21 bytes followed by three alignment zero bytes. Metadata immediately after those exact boundaries remains counted.",
    "native_catalog": "The existing generated catalog is retained as one source-level data owner. Ordinary initialized definitions emit its complete original contiguous data run in source order in all three regions. This reconstruction treats the homogeneous catalog as a legitimate data-only translation unit; no stronger claim about original filenames is made. Every object address, physical extent, initializer and relocation target is preserved; no padding, qualification or regional-bound changes are introduced."
  }
